# Caddy reverse proxy — terminates TLS in front of the API (brief §3, on-prem).
#
# The API speaks HTTP Basic, which must ride TLS; this gives the stack a safe HTTPS
# URL from one `docker compose --profile tls up -d`, instead of an SSH tunnel.
#
# Default is the ON-PREM case: a private hostname with Caddy's internal CA
# (`tls internal`, a self-signed cert). Reach it over the mine LAN/VPN and trust the
# CA once, or accept the browser prompt. For a PUBLIC domain with a real certificate,
# set MM_TLS_EMAIL in .env: compose passes it here as MM_TLS_ISSUER, and `tls <email>`
# makes Caddy provision Let's Encrypt. Neither needs this file edited, so an upgrade,
# which replaces it, keeps the choice.
#
# MM_SITE_DOMAIN may list several addresses, comma-separated: "10.20.0.5, minebox".
#
# Caddy connects to the API over the compose network (service name `api`), so the
# API's own published port can stay bound to localhost — only Caddy faces the world.

{$MM_SITE_DOMAIN:localhost} {
	encode gzip
	# Streaming-friendly: the dashboard's live updates use Server-Sent Events.
	reverse_proxy api:8000 {
		flush_interval -1
	}
	tls {$MM_TLS_ISSUER:internal}
}
